and an unknown asymptotic setting that allows for an apparently infinite zo\om.</span></p></body>)/Rect[432.253 267.118 573.404 339.515]/Subj(Text Box)/Subtype/FreeText/T( )/Type/Annot>>endobj43 0 obj<</BS<</W 0>>/CA 1/Contents(     None.)/DA(0 0 0 RG 0 0 0 rg /Helv 10 Tf)/DS(font: Helvetica,sans-serif 10.0pt; text-align:left; color:#000000 )/F 4/M(D:20240918170939-03'00')/NM(bb138ce29a0f43518600d4a0c15c9228)/Page 1/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:24.3.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"  style="font-size:10.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:norma\l;font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-stretch:normal"><p dir="ltr"><span style="font-family:Helvetica">     None.</span></p></body>)/Rect[273.661 275.412 340.261 287.612]/Subj(Text Box)/Subtype/FreeText/T( )/Type/Annot>>endobj44 0 obj<</BS<</W 0>>/CA 1/Contents(In 1994, hobbyist astronomer Victor Oliver West discovered with the tool a strange object, a celestial body later identified as an asteroid with approximately 373 miles in diameter and a perfectly spherical shape, which, regardless of the direction where \the scientist pointed the telescope, always remained perfectly centered. By researching with material given by the Laniakea Observatory personnel, from the North District, he found an asteroid with the same descriptions in the center of the biggest astron\omical void of this universe: The Gloˆvs Void, and, flabbergasted, tried to study the movement of the body, later figuring out that, in actuality, this movement was aligned with the telescopeês field of vision, always following, and always in its center. \Believing to be suffering hallucinations, West tried to clean the tool, thinking he could maybe be reading the data wrong, and the dot was some kind of smudge, and accidentally discovered the setting, and becoming interesting in what it could do and, spec\ially, what relation it had with the object.  The setting consists of using the fingers directly on the lens and dragging in a zoom movement, like a smartphone and, after doing this, West disappeared, along with the telescope. In truth he was teleported t\o The Wanderer \(the name he gave to the body before his disappearance\) and its whereabouts were traced through uncommon pathways ever since, with its last seen spot being close to Saturnês third moon, Ganymede. Since then, Westês whereabouts are unknown\, and, if ever found, he will be dead, due to unknown causes.)/DA(0 0 0 RG 0 0 0 rg /Arial 10 Tf)/DS(font: Arial,sans-serif 10.0pt; text-align:left; color:#000000 )/F 4/M(D:20240918171236-03'00')/NM(d7acb930cdb148c288da8a71ac95f1c3)/Page 1/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:24.3.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"  style="font-size:10.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:norma\l;font-family:Arial;font-stretch:normal"><p dir="ltr"><span style="text-align:justify">In 1994, hobbyist astronomer Victor Oliver West discovered with the tool a strange object, a celestial body later identified as an asteroid with approximately 373 miles\ in diameter and a perfectly spherical shape, which, regardless of the direction where the scientist pointed the telescope, always remained perfectly centered. By researching with material given by the Laniakea Observatory personnel, from the North Distri\ct, he found an asteroid with the same descriptions in the center of the biggest astronomical void of this universe: The Glo&#246;vs Void, and, flabbergasted, tried to study the movement of the body, later figuring out that, in actuality, this movement wa\s aligned with the telescope&#8217;s field of vision, always following, and always in its center. Believing to be suffering hallucinations, West tried to clean the tool, thinking he could maybe be reading the data wrong, and the dot was some kind of smudg\e, and accidentally discovered the setting, and becoming interesting in what it could do and, specially, what relation it had with the object.  The setting consists of using the fingers directly on the lens and dragging in a zoom movement, like a smartpho\ne and, after doing this, West disappeared, along with the telescope. In truth he was teleported to The Wanderer \(the name he gave to the body before his disappearance\) and its whereabouts were traced through uncommon pathways ever since, with its last \seen spot being close to Saturn&#8217;s third moon, Ganymede. Since then, West&#8217;s whereabouts are unknown, and, if ever found, he will be dead, due to unknown causes.</span></p></body>)/Rect[45.3301 40.864 571.302 206.874]/Subj(Text Box)/Subtype/FreeText/T( )/Type/Annot>>endobj45 0 obj<</BS<</W 0>>/CA 1/Contents( 2000)/DA(0 0 0 RG 0 0 0 rg 0 Tc 0 Tw 100 Tz 0 TL 0 Ts 0 Tr /Helv 21.8 Tf)/DS(font: Helvetica,sans-serif 21.8pt; text-align:left; color:#000000 )/F 4/M(D:20240912132418-03'00')/NM(1136aaeffc294a48afa27816b1ee9af3)/Page 2/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:24.3.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"  style="font-size:21.8pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:norma\l;font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-stretch:normal"><p dir="ltr"><span style="font-family:Helvetica"> 2000</span></p></body>)/Rect[17.301 674.202 180.873 699.612]/Subj(Text Box)/Subtype/FreeText/T( )/Type/Annot>>endobj46 0 obj<</BS<</W 0>>/CA 1/Contents(   As)/DA(0 0 0 RG 0 0 0 rg 0 Tc 0 Tw 100 Tz 0 TL 0 Ts 0 Tr /Helv 21.8 Tf)/DS(font: Helvetica,sans-serif 21.8pt; text-align:left; color:#000000 )/F 4/M(D:20240912131544-03'00')/NM(e7a7212f000b47d5b3601cb1cf455d44)/Page 2/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:24.3.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"  style="font